+       distance budgets (accelerate_until, decelerate_after, intersect_distance,
+       junction entry speeds) then come out correct in DISTANCE and TIME, and
+       the stepper is free to shape the velocity inside that budget.
+
+       For the quintic ("smootherstep") ramp used by st_prep_buffer():
+
+           v(tau) = v0 + dv * (6*tau^5 - 15*tau^4 + 10*tau^3),  tau = t/T
+
+       the integral of the basis polynomial over [0,1] is exactly 1/2, so the
+       ramp's mean velocity is exactly (v0 + v1)/2 -- identical to a linear
+       ramp of the same duration. That is what makes this substitution legal:
+       a quintic ramp and a straight-line ramp covering the same distance take
+       the SAME time T = 2*S/(v0+v1). Only the derivatives differ:
+
+           peak accel = (15/8)   * dv/T    = 1.875   * a_eff
+           peak jerk  = (10/V3)  * dv/T^2  = 5.77350 * a_eff^2/dv
+
+       (both exact; d/dtau of the basis is 30*tau^2*(tau-1)^2, peaking at
+       tau = 0.5, and its second derivative peaks at tau = (3 -/+ V3)/6.)
+
+       Inverting those two expressions against the axis-limited acceleration
+       and jerk gives two simultaneous ceilings on a_eff, and the binding one
+       wins. Using dv ~ programmed_rate follows the same symmetrical
+       full-ramp-from-rest convention the previous implementation used:
+
+           a_eff <= max_acceleration * 8/15
+           a_eff <= sqrt(jerk * programmed_rate / (10/V3))
+
+       The first dominates at high feed rate (long ramps, jerk is cheap), the
+       second at low feed rate (short ramps, where an accel-limited quintic
+       would demand unbounded jerk). Both are closed form -- no iteration and
+       no regime test, unlike the trapezoidal-jerk profile this replaces.
+
+       For reference, the trapezoidal-jerk formula previously used here derated
+       by a factor that ran from 2.0 (pure double-jerk ramp, no constant-accel
+       plateau) down to 1.0 (long plateau, jerk fillets negligible). The
+       quintic's 1.875 sits inside that range, so effective acceleration on an
+       already-tuned machine changes only modestly: slightly lower at high feed
